 "verdict": "confirmed",
 "derivation": "Late/Vulgar Latin plattus 'flat' is a borrowing of Greek platús, not an inherited Latin cognate — the geminate -tt- and the a-vocalism give it away (inherited *pl̥t- would have yielded Latin lat-/plat- as in latus, planta). It is the source of Italian piatto, Old French plat, Old Provençal plat, Spanish plato/plata, and hence of the whole modern 'flat/plate' family. Klein sets out the parallel case on p.1192: 'VL. *plattea (whence also It. piazza, OProvenç. plasa, Catal. plassa, Sp. plaza, Port. praça), fr. L. platea, „open space, street“, fr. Gk. πλατεῖα (ὁδός)'. Nourai's box is therefore correct, though 'Latin' here means the spoken Latin of the Empire, not Classical Latin.",
